    "summary_one_sentence": "A dedicated Parisian nursing home director transforms her facility and her life by embracing the revolutionary 'Humanitude' care method alongside a terminally ill Japanese playwright.",
    "summary_short": "Marie-Lou Fontaine serves as the director of a suburban Paris nursing home, determined to improve the quality of life for her residents. Facing intense skepticism and resistance from her staff, she decides to implement 'Humanitude,' an innovative approach centered on compassion and personal connection. Her journey takes a profound turn when she meets Mari Morisaki, a terminally ill Japanese playwright admitted to the facility. Together, the two women forge an extraordinary bond that challenges institutional constraints and turns caregiving into a powerful act of dignity.",
    "summary_long": "In the suburbs of Paris, nursing home director Marie-Lou Fontaine struggles against rigid administrative standards and a routine care system. Driven by a desire to restore dignity to eldercare, Marie-Lou introduces the 'Humanitude' philosophy, which emphasizes eye contact, gentleness, and direct communication over clinical speed. Despite severe resistance from staff members set in their ways, she stands firm in her commitment to change. The arrival of Mari Morisaki, a terminally ill Japanese playwright, becomes the central catalyst for a monumental shift within the institution. Mari’s artistic sensibilities and perspective on mortality resonate deeply with Marie-Lou, sparking an unexpected friendship between the two. Through their shared efforts, the nursing home gradually sheds its sterile atmosphere to become a vibrant sanctuary of empathy and warmth. The staff eventually embraces the philosophy as they witness its transformative effect on the residents' well-being. Ultimately, Marie-Lou and Mari turn the facility into a symbol of resistance against institutional limits, demonstrating the profound power of human connection in the final stages of life.",
    "synopsis": "All of a Sudden follows Marie-Lou Fontaine, a suburban Paris nursing home director who seeks to revolutionize eldercare by adopting the 'Humanitude' methodology. Challenging institutional norms and facing strong pushback from her resistant staff, Marie-Lou's mission gains new clarity when she meets Mari Morisaki, a terminally ill Japanese playwright. Their profound bond transforms both the facility and its community into a symbol of empathy, dignity, and resistance against systemic boundaries.",
